These two `useEffect` hooks form a chain: the first effect (line 127) sets `cameraPermissionStatus` via `refreshCameraPermissionStatus`, and the second effect (line 135) reacts to `cameraPermissionStatus` changing to `RESULTS.DENIED` by calling `askForPermissions`. This causes two render cycles when the modal opens with denied permissions.

Combine these into a single effect that both refreshes the permission status and handles the denied case:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!isVisible) {
        return;
    }
    CameraPermission?.getCameraPermissionStatus?.()
        .then((status) => {
            setCameraPermissionStatus(status);
            if (status === RESULTS.DENIED) {
                askForPermissions();
            }
        })
        .catch(() => setCameraPermissionStatus(RESULTS.UNAVAILABLE));
}, [isVisible, askForPermissions]);
```

The OAuth feed expiration effect (line 58) calls `setFeedRefreshComplete()` which sets `isRefreshing` to `null` in Onyx. This state change then triggers the Plaid/isRefreshing effect (line 50) to call `Navigation.closeRHPFlow()`. This is an effect chain where one effect's state update triggers another.

The OAuth effect should handle navigation directly instead of relying on the intermediate state update to trigger another effect:

```tsx
// OAuth feeds: expiration updates after bank re-authentication completes
useEffect(() => {
    if (prevFeedExpiration === undefined || prevFeedExpiration === feedExpiration || !isRefreshing) {
        return;
    }
    setFeedRefreshComplete();
    Navigation.closeRHPFlow();
}, [prevFeedExpiration, feedExpiration, isRefreshing]);
```

This eliminates the chain and makes the OAuth completion path self-contained.

