Converting `refreshSuccessHandled` from `useRef` to `useState` creates an effect chain: this large effect sets `refreshSuccessHandled` via `setRefreshSuccessHandled(true)` (line 154), which triggers a re-render, which causes the `isRefreshComplete` useMemo (line 76) to evaluate to `true`, which then triggers the separate `useEffect` at line 116 to fire and call `onSuccess?.()`. This is a classic effect chain where one effect's state update triggers another effect through an intermediate derived value.

Instead of going through the state -> useMemo -> effect chain, call `onSuccess?.()` directly in this effect when the refresh connection flow succeeds and the feed is healthy:

```ts
if (!refreshSuccessHandled.current) {
    refreshSuccessHandled.current = true;
    onSuccess?.();
}
```

This keeps `refreshSuccessHandled` as a `useRef` (avoiding the extra re-render) and eliminates the indirect chain, while still guarding against double invocation.

