The `useEffect` at line 100 (bounds clamping) depends on `focusedIndex`, which can be updated by the `useEffect` at line 77 (key tracking after reorder) via `setFocusedIndex(newIndex)` at line 96. This creates an effect chain: when `itemKeys` changes, Effect 2 may update `focusedIndex`, which then triggers Effect 3 to run, producing an extra render cycle.

Both effects handle the same concern -- adjusting `focusedIndex` in response to list item changes -- and can be combined into a single effect:

```typescript
useEffect(() => {
    if (!isActive) {
        prevKeysRef.current = itemKeys;
        return;
    }
    const prevKeys = prevKeysRef.current;
    prevKeysRef.current = itemKeys;

    let newIndex = focusedIndex;

    // Track focused item across reorders
    if (newIndex >= 0 && newIndex < prevKeys.length) {
        const focusedKey = prevKeys.at(newIndex);
        if (focusedKey && !(newIndex < itemKeys.length && itemKeys.at(newIndex) === focusedKey)) {
            const keyIndex = itemKeys.indexOf(focusedKey);
            if (keyIndex >= 0) {
                newIndex = keyIndex;
            }
        }
    }

    // Clamp to bounds
    if (newIndex > itemKeys.length - 1) {
        newIndex = Math.max(itemKeys.length - 1, -1);
    }

    if (newIndex !== focusedIndex) {
        setFocusedIndex(newIndex);
    }
}, [itemKeys, focusedIndex, setFocusedIndex, isActive]);
```

