The flag reset logic creates a problematic state management pattern. After processing a deep link, all flags are reset to `false`, which means the next callback from Onyx will set them back to `true` and potentially trigger `processPendingDeepLinkIfReady()` again even without a new deep link URL. This creates an implicit chain of state updates.

Additionally, if a new deep link arrives while processing the current one, the flags will be reset and the new link may be processed with stale data.

**Suggested fix:**
Remove the flag reset logic and use a simpler pattern - only check flags on the first deep link, then rely on the module-level state being kept up-to-date by Onyx subscriptions:

```typescript
function processPendingDeepLinkIfReady() {
    if (\!pendingDeepLinkUrl) {
        return;
    }

    // Wait for all subscriptions to be ready (at least one callback fired)
    if (\!hasOnboardingPurposeReceived || \!hasOnboardingCompanySizeReceived || \!hasOnboardingInitialPathReceived || \!hasReportsReceived) {
        return;
    }

    const urlToProcess = pendingDeepLinkUrl;
    pendingDeepLinkUrl = null;

    const isCurrentlyAuthenticated = hasAuthToken();
    openReportFromDeepLink(urlToProcess, currentOnboardingPurposeSelected, currentOnboardingCompanySize, onboardingInitialPath, allReports, isCurrentlyAuthenticated);

    // Flags are NOT reset - future deep links will use up-to-date values from active subscriptions
}
```

This code creates a chain of useEffects where one effect's state update triggers another effect. The `needToClearError` state is set in one effect (lines 88-99), which then triggers logic in another effect (lines 100-106).

**Suggested fix**: Restructure the logic to avoid interdependent effects. Consider combining the error handling logic or using a single effect that handles both clearing errors and resetting the state.

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!account?.errors) {
        return;
    }

    clearAccountMessages();
    setFormError({});
}, [account?.errors]);
```

These two useEffects form a chain where the first effect's state update (`setSelectedApproverType`) triggers the second effect. This creates complex dependencies and unnecessary renders.

**Suggested fix:** Combine the logic into a single effect or derive values directly:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (hasAutoAppliedRef.current || approverTypes.length \!== 1 || hasNavigatedToAddApproverRef.current) {
        return;
    }
    hasAutoAppliedRef.current = true;
    const firstOption = approverTypes.at(0)?.keyForList;
    if (firstOption) {
        setSelectedApproverType(firstOption);
        // Call changeApprover in next tick or handle navigation directly
        setTimeout(() => changeApprover(), 0);
    }
}, [approverTypes, changeApprover]);
```

This eliminates the chain and makes the logic flow clearer.

This useEffect creates redundant logic with the existing useEffect at line 184-189, which already watches `isExpanded` and calls `searchTransactions()`. Having two useEffects that watch the same state and perform the same action causes duplicate API calls and unnecessary renders.

**Suggested fix**: Remove this new useEffect entirely. The existing useEffect at line 184-189 already handles refetching when `isExpanded` becomes true. If you need different behavior for the `newTransactionID` case vs the general expand case, refactor the existing useEffect logic instead of creating a second one:

```typescript
useEffect(() => {
    if (!isExpanded) {
        return;
    }
    // This already handles both cases: when expanding AND when newTransactionID changes
    searchTransactions();
}, [newTransactionID, isExpanded, searchTransactions]);
```
